{"kind":"propose","text":"Set the ordinary per-package timeout to 60 seconds instead of 30. Keep -short, package serialization, all current command-level correctness and security cases, same-stream failure/timing reporting, CI -count=1, and the \u003c10-second kernel/app targets unchanged. Evidence: independent uncached measurements put cmd/gs at 27.63s and MCP at 24.16s, while same-host test variance today reached 1.69x; a 30-second panic cliff has only 1.09x margin for gs. Sixty seconds gives roughly 2.2x margin while remaining a bounded fail-fast policy and staying well below the explicitly rejected loose 120-second default. Consequence: a genuinely hung package fails within one minute, while ordinary load variance does not create misleading goroutine-dump failures. This is the requested review repair and the only changed condition.","body":{"choice":"Use a 60-second ordinary per-package timeout while retaining every current short-lane case and all other fast-lane conditions.","consequences":"Avoids measured load-variance flakes without weakening correctness coverage; true hangs fail within one minute; the 30-second condition is superseded, the \u003c10-second kernel/app condition remains."}}